---
import Feature from '../../components/Feature.astro'
import Snapshot from '../../components/Snapshot.astro'
import { FEATURES } from '../../features'
import FeaturePage from '../../layouts/FeaturePage.astro'
import type { PageMeta } from '../../meta'

export const meta = {
  description: FEATURES.security.sentence,
  sources: [
    'docs/security.md',
    'docs/security/credentials.md',
    'docs/security/process-and-paths.md',
    'docs/security/instruction-review.md',
    'docs/security/node-plugin-security.md',
    'docs/security/plugin-bundles.md',
    'docs/security/audit.md',
    'docs/telemetry.md',
    'docs/data-layer/backup-and-retention.md',
    'docs/managed-agents/accounts.md',
  ],
  checked: '9aaaaf7d3',
} satisfies PageMeta
---

<FeaturePage feature="security" meta={meta} related={['agents', 'plugins', 'nodes']} docs={{ href: '/docs/security/the-trust-model/', label: 'Read the trust model' }}>
  <Snapshot slot="picture" id="permissions" />

  <Feature number={1} title="Agents get a token for their own task" href="/docs/security/the-trust-model/" link="The trust model">
    <p>
      An agent gets a token that reaches only its own task's routes on the Node and can't spend a credential acorn
      holds. Every process acorn starts gets an environment built from an allowlist, never a copy of the Node's, so it
      doesn't get acorn's encryption key or your GitHub token.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={2} title="Files and instructions are checked" href="/docs/security/files-processes-and-project-configuration/" link="Files, processes, and project configuration">
    <p>
      acorn's own file reads and writes stay inside the data folder and the worktrees, and follow symlinks before
      they decide, so a link a branch adds to <code>~/.ssh</code> is refused. Committed project configuration that
      runs code needs your review before it runs, and again when it changes. Two settings, off by default, check{' '}
      <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code>, and skills before each agent turn: one for hidden characters,
      one for changes you haven't reviewed.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={3} title="Plugins run sandboxed, with the grants you accept" href="/docs/security/plugin-trust-and-permissions/" link="Plugin trust and permissions">
    <p>
      An installed plugin's client code runs in a sandbox and draws only with acorn's components or inside its own
      frame. Its Node half runs in a worker that gets only the network, file, environment, and process grants its
      manifest lists. Each device hashes a plugin's code itself and asks you before it runs those exact bytes.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={4} title="Where your credentials live" href="/docs/security/credentials-and-secrets/" link="Credentials and secrets">
    <p>
      The credentials you connect, such as GitHub's, are encrypted on the Node, write-only, and never sent back in an
      answer, a log, or an event. A backup blanks them. Claude Code and Codex keep their logins in their own
      folders, and acorn never handles those tokens.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={5} title="An audit log, and telemetry off" href="/docs/security/the-audit-log/" link="The audit log">
    <p>
      <strong>Settings → Audit log</strong> lists 90 days of security decisions: pairings, devices revoked, secrets
      changed, plugins installed or turned off, and backups. Telemetry is off by default, and nothing leaves the
      machine unless you turn it on and add somewhere to send it.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature number={6} title="What acorn doesn't protect against" href="/docs/security/what-acorn-doesnt-protect-against/" link="What acorn doesn't protect against">
    <p>
      An agent runs shell commands as you, so it can read what you can, including the rest of acorn's data folder.{' '}
      <strong>Keep agents out of Acorn's data folder</strong> closes that for Claude Code sessions. It's off by
      default, and Codex can't enforce it. acorn doesn't defend a Node against root or other users on its machine,
      and doesn't sandbox the plugins built into the app.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Fragment slot="terminal">
    <p>
      The terminal client runs the app and its connection to the Node in one process, so the line the desktop draws
      between two processes is a line between modules there. It keeps the device token out of every module that
      draws, and still runs each installed plugin in a worker of its own.{' '}
      <a href="/docs/security/the-trust-model/">The trust model</a>
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</FeaturePage>
